\description{
Constructs a covariate forest plot from a \code{\link[=df_forest]{df_forest()}} result (or any
object satisfying the \code{forest_stats} contract). Renders per-row point
estimates with horizontal CI lines, optional shaded equivalence band, and
a vertical reference line, one facet row per covariate name. The
\code{est_label}, \code{lo_label}, and \code{hi_label} columns are assembled into the
right-hand secondary y-axis text (\code{"1.29 (1.19, 1.41)"}) under a header
built from the container's \code{ci} (e.g. \code{"Estimate (90\% CI)"}), so each
row's estimate sits beside its point. The header is the plot subtitle,
right-aligned so it sits on the top edge of the panels above the column;
change it with \code{+ ggplot2::labs(subtitle = "Ratio (90\% CI)")}. The
rendered metric labels the x-axis, and the plot has no title by default.

Rows are drawn in the order they appear in \code{stats$stats}, top to bottom
(see the "Row order" section of \code{\link[=df_forest]{df_forest()}}); reorder that frame to
change the plot. Each row is its own y position, so covariate level
labels may repeat across panels (\code{Low} / \code{High} under two covariates).
The \code{layout} column selects the rows for the chosen reference layout (see
\code{ref_layout}).

Rows flagged \code{is_ref} (the reference copies in each covariate panel, or the
reference panel) draw through the \code{ref_point} style role
(an open circle by default) and show the point estimate alone in the
estimate column, since their CI collapses onto the estimate. A row whose
level label repeats its covariate name (the reference panel) has no
left-axis tick or label, since the strip already carries the name.

Returns a plain \code{ggplot} object so callers can compose additional layers,
themes, and scales with the standard \code{+} operator.

Most users will reach this function indirectly via \code{\link[=plot_forest]{plot_forest()}}.
Call \code{plot_build_forest()} directly when working from a manually-
constructed or cached \code{forest_stats} object — for example, plotting a
precomputed result from disk or a custom pipeline that produces
compatible columns and config.
